      "Parvati's grief was absolute and terrifying. She threatened to dissolve all of creation in her sorrow. The worlds shook. Shiva, finally understanding what he had done, sent his ganas north to find the head of the first living being they found sleeping with its head pointing north. They returned with the head of an elephant. Shiva placed the elephant's head on the boy's body and breathed life into him. The boy lived — and became Ganesha, lord of all the ganas, elephant-headed, the remover of obstacles.",
      "His broken tusk: when the sage Vyasa wanted to dictate the entire Mahabharata — 100,000 verses, the largest poem ever composed — he needed a scribe who could write without stopping. Ganesha agreed — on one condition: Vyasa must never pause in his dictation. Vyasa agreed — with his own condition: that Ganesha must never write a word he did not fully understand. They began. Ganesha's pen broke — without hesitation, he snapped off his own tusk and used it as a pen, continuing without a break. When Vyasa reached a passage of great complexity, he composed it so profoundly that Ganesha had to pause and truly understand before writing — giving Vyasa a moment to compose the next passage. The Mahabharata was written this way: God's wisdom and the Creator's poetry in perfect dialogue.",
