      "The story of Nachiketa from the Katha Upanishad: a young brahmin boy named Nachiketa watched his father perform a yajna with cows too old and sick to give milk — a hollow gift, giving what is worthless to God. Disturbed, Nachiketa asked: 'Father, to whom will you give me?' The father, irritated by the interruption, said in anger: 'I give you to Death (Yama).' Nachiketa took his father's words as a divine instruction and walked directly to Yama's abode.",
      "When he arrived, Yama was absent for three days. Nachiketa waited without food or shelter for three days and nights at the door of Death. When Yama returned, he was moved by the boy's patience and honoured him with three boons. For the first two boons, Nachiketa asked for his father's peace and the secret of a sacred fire ritual. For the third boon — the question that changes everything — Nachiketa asked: 'Some say the soul continues after death. Others say it does not. Tell me the truth.' Yama was startled. He tried to dissuade Nachiketa with wealth beyond imagination, beautiful women, vast kingdoms, a life of pleasure. Nachiketa refused every offer: 'What are all these things to a man who knows death is coming? Tell me the truth.' And Yama, deeply moved, taught him the secret of the Atman — the immortal self that is never born, never dies, never cut by weapons, never burned by fire. This teaching, given by Death himself, became the Katha Upanishad."
