      "Varuna's thousand eyes are the stars — the night sky is his form, watching everything that happens across the earth. No action escapes his witness. His weapon is the Pasha (cosmic noose) — which binds those who violate cosmic order. But Varuna is not simply a cosmic policeman. He is the god who answers the prayer of repentance.",
      "The Varuna Suktas in the Rigveda — hymns addressed to Varuna by the sage Vasishtha — are among the most beautiful, most human prayers in all world literature. Vasishtha says: 'Varuna, whatever sin I have committed against the gods, against my ancestors, against my friends — whether in wakefulness or in dream — whether knowingly or unknowingly — release me from it, as a calf is released from its tether. I am bound by my own mistakes. Untie me.' And Varuna answers — not with punishment but with the loosening of the knots, with forgiveness, with the restoration of the relationship between the mortal and the cosmic.",
      "As the Puranic period developed, Varuna's cosmic sovereignty gradually transformed — he became the ruler of the oceans, the deity of the deep waters, the keeper of the cosmic waters from which all life arose. His ancient grandeur as the keeper of Rita faded in popular worship but never in the Vedas — where his voice still speaks of the intimate relationship between the human conscience and the cosmic order."
